X: 1
T: El Choclo
C: Ángel Villoldo, 1903
O: Argentina
R: Tango
N: "The ear of corn". One of the oldest tangos still played everywhere; it
N: was premiered in 1903 at the restaurant El Americano in Buenos Aires,
N: billed as a "danza criolla" because the owner disliked tango.
N: Melody and chords from John Chambers' transcription (trillian.mit.edu,
N: Argentina/Choclo), checked bar by bar against the flute setting on
N: flutetunes.com, which plays the same notes and writes the grace notes as
N: sixteenth triplets. Note values doubled from the 2/4 source so the moving
N: value is an eighth. Transposed down a tone from A minor / A major to
N: G minor / G major to fit the recorder (range C#4 to A5).
N: In bar 9 of the trio flutetunes has D5 where Chambers repeats bar 1 (B4).
N: Played straight through A-B-C; the source's D.C. back to the start is left out.
N: The accompaniment uses a tango 3-3-2 pattern (%%MIDI gchord).
L: 1/8
Q: 1/4=120
%%MIDI gchord bzzczzcz
M: 4/4
K: Gm
